## Solution

Let the sphere have radius $r=1$ centered at $O$. Place $O$ at the origin and let the common axis $\hat n$ point from $O$ toward $P$. Since the segment $PQ$ passes through $O$, the drones lie on opposite sides: $P = p\,\hat n$ and $Q = -q\,\hat n$ with $p,q>1$.

**Tangent geometry.** For an external point at distance $d$ from the center of a unit sphere, a tangent line touches the sphere at a point $T$ satisfying $OT\perp PT$, i.e. $T\cdot(T-P)=0$, so $|T|^2 = T\cdot P$, giving $T\cdot\hat n = 1/d$. The two tangent lines from the point lie in a plane through the axis and make a full angle $2\alpha$ with $\sin\alpha = 1/d$.

**For Drone P:** $2\alpha = 45^\circ$, so $\alpha = 22.5^\circ$ and $1/p = \sin 22.5^\circ = \cos 67.5^\circ$. The contact points $P_1,P_2$ lie on the latitude circle at polar angle $\theta_P = 67.5^\circ$ from $\hat n$, and they are antipodal on that circle (azimuths differing by $180^\circ$).

**For Drone Q:** $2\beta = 30^\circ$, so $\beta = 15^\circ$ and $1/q = \sin 15^\circ = \cos 75^\circ$. Since $Q$ is on the $-\hat n$ side, its contact points satisfy $T\cdot\hat n = -1/q = -\cos 75^\circ = \cos 105^\circ$, so $Q_1,Q_2$ lie on the latitude circle at polar angle $\theta_Q = 105^\circ$ from $\hat n$, again antipodal on that circle.

**Minimizing the arc.** The orientation of each tangent plane about the axis is free, so the azimuths of $P_2$ and $Q_2$ can be chosen independently (choosing which antipodal point is "$P_2$" shifts azimuth by $180^\circ$, which lets us select either sign of $\cos\Delta\phi$). The great-circle (central) angle $\gamma$ between a point at $(\theta_P,\phi_P)$ and a point at $(\theta_Q,\phi_Q)$ satisfies

$$\cos\gamma = \cos\theta_P\cos\theta_Q + \sin\theta_P\sin\theta_Q\cos\Delta\phi.$$

To minimize the arc we maximize $\cos\gamma$ by taking $\cos\Delta\phi = 1$ (align the two points on the same meridian):

$$\cos\gamma_{\max} = \cos(\theta_Q-\theta_P) = \cos(105^\circ-67.5^\circ)=\cos 37.5^\circ.$$

Hence $\gamma_{\min} = 37.5^\circ$. This is achievable: rotate both tangent planes to the same azimuth and pick the contact points on the same side; they then lie on a single great circle through the axis, separated by exactly $37.5^\circ$.

**Arc length.** With unit radius,

$$\widehat{P_2Q_2}_{\min} = 37.5^\circ\cdot\frac{\pi}{180^\circ} = \frac{75^\circ}{360^\circ}\pi = \frac{5\pi}{24}.$$

The minimum possible length of the arc $P_2Q_2$ is $\boxed{\dfrac{5\pi}{24}}$ megameters.

